spans.s:
1. स्व॑ mf(आ॑)n. own, one's own, my own, thy own, his own, her own, our own, their own &c. (referring to all three persons accord. to context, often ibc., but generally declinable like the pronominal सर्व, e.g. स्वस्मै dat. स्वस्मात् abl. [optionally in abl. loc. sing. nom. pl., e.g. तं स्वाद् आस्याद् असृजत्, ‘he created him from his own mouth’, Mn. i, 94];
and always like शिव when used substantively [see below];
sometimes used loosely for ‘my’, ‘thy’, ‘his’, ‘our’ [e.g. राजा भ्रातरं स्व-गृहम् प्रेषयाम्-आस, ‘the king sent his brother to his (i.e. the brother's) house’];
in the oblique cases it is used as a reflexive pronoun = आत्मन्, e.g. स्वं दूषयति, ‘he defiles himself’;
स्वं निन्दन्ति, ‘they blame themselves’), RV. &c. &c.
